\revresponse{ We thank the reviewer for this comment and we apologise
  that it was not explicitly addressed in our previous reply. The
  sentence the reviewer quotes belongs to the Bitcoin Proof-of-Work
  paragraph of the experimental evaluation, and the ``discrepancies''
  it refers to are specifically the differences between the curves
  obtained by running the reference PRISM model and our generated
  model.
  \smallskip

  We did not use simulation by preference: we used it because, for
  the two larger protocols in our benchmark set (Bitcoin PoW and
  Hybrid Casper), the underlying state space is prohibitively large
  for exact probabilistic model checking. This is the standard
  choice in PRISM for this class of models, and it is indeed the
  choice also made in the papers from which these benchmarks are
  taken. Both the reference model and our generated model are
  therefore analysed by PRISM's discrete-event simulation engine
  (statistical model checking), and the ``discrepancies'' between
  the resulting curves are the expected Monte Carlo sampling noise
  between independent simulation runs: running the same model twice
  already produces slightly different curves, and running two
  probabilistically equivalent models produces curves that are
  indistinguishable up to that noise. This is what we meant by
  ``inherent variations in the simulation-based calculation of
  probability''; the phrase is perhaps too terse, and we are happy
  to expand it in the paper if the reviewer prefers (see the
  optional edit proposed in the ``Changes'' block below).
  \smallskip

  For the remaining smaller benchmarks (thinkteam, simple
  peer-to-peer, synchronous leader election, dining cryptographers),
  the state space is tractable and PRISM performs full
  probabilistic model checking with its default engines; no
  simulation is involved there, and the results agree with the
  reference models in the precise sense we adopt in
  Section~\ref{sec:bench} (see our reply to \textbf{[R2.18/R3.3]}).
  \smallskip

  Regarding the reviewer's specific suggestion to use PRISM's
  \texttt{-exact} option (rational arithmetic): we did not run
  \texttt{prism -exact} in this evaluation. For Bitcoin~PoW and
  Hybrid Casper the suggestion is not applicable, as
  \texttt{-exact} is incompatible with the simulation engine and
  those models cannot be handled exactly in any case. For the
  smaller benchmarks, PRISM's default floating-point computation
  already produces values that match the reference probabilities
  well within the precision reported in the benchmark papers, so
  we did not see a concrete reason to switch to rational
  arithmetic. We are, however, happy to add such experiments to the
  paper (on the smaller benchmarks where it is feasible) if the
  reviewer considers them necessary for the publication.}

