  values), and that we deliberately do \emph{not} aim for
  bisimilarity or semantic equivalence at the model level. The
  reason is that our projection targets a structured fragment of
  PRISM and does not try to reproduce the exact syntactic form or
  the full range of nondeterminism present in some hand-written
  PRISM models; bisimilarity would therefore typically fail for
  reasons that are irrelevant to the benchmarks we care about,
  whereas the relevant quantitative analyses still agree.
  \smallskip

  We also note (to avoid confusion with our earlier reply) that the
  Remark at the end of Section~\ref{sec:proj} is a \emph{different}
  discussion: it addresses the asymmetry of
  Theorem~\ref{thm:epp} with respect to projecting-after-reducing
  and reducing-after-projecting (a point originally raised by
  Reviewer~1), and it mentions probabilistic bisimulation only as a
  candidate notion for a future strengthening of that theorem --
  not as the form of comparison used in the experimental
  evaluation.}

